{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_10","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"browser-cmd batch <<'EOF'\nnavigate https://www.allrecipes.com/recipe/15821/aunt-teens-creamy-chocolate-fudge/\ntext\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"navigate: Navigated. | URL: https://www.allrecipes.com/recipe/15821/aunt-teens-creamy-chocolate-fudge/ | Title: Aunt Teen’s Creamy Chocolate Fudge Recipe\n<browser trusted=\"no\">\nURL: https://www.allrecipes.com/recipe/15821/aunt-teens-creamy-chocolate-fudge/\n\n[Skipped site header, footer — `text full=true` reads them]\nDesserts\nCandy Recipes\nFudge Recipes\nChocolate\n# Aunt Teen’s Creamy Chocolate Fudge\n4.7\n(2,465)\n1,951 Reviews\n113 Photos\nJump to Recipe\nThis is the best fudge recipe ever! It’s my aunt’s recipe, passed down through the family. It’s better than any fudge I’ve ever had at the Jersey shore and easy enough to whip up in 15 minutes or so.\nSubmitted by\nKelly Phillips\nUpdated on January 22, 2026\nTested by\nAllrecipes Test Kitchen\nSave\nRate\nPrint\nShare\nAdd Photo\n113\nPrep Time:\n10 mins\nCook Time:\n20 mins\nAdditional Time:\n2 hrs\nTotal Time:\n2 hrs 30 mins\nServings:\n48\nYield:\n1 (8x8-inch) pan\nJump to Nutrition Facts\nReady to make the best fudge of your life? You’ve come to the right place! You’ll come back to this creamy fudge recipe again and again.\nKim's Cooking Now\n## How to Store and Freeze Fudge\nStore the fudge in an airtight container (with wax paper between layers) at room temperature for one to two weeks. There’s no need to refrigerate fudge.\nTo freeze fudge, wrap it tightly in wax paper, then wrap it again in aluminum foil. Place the foil-wrapped fudge in a freezer-safe container or wrap it in another layer of foil. Freeze for up to three months. Thaw in the refrigerator overnight.\nAunt Teen’s Creamy Chocolate Fudge | Photo by Kari.\nKari\n## Allrecipes Community Tips and Praise\n“I have made this fudge several times over the years and have followed the recipe to a tee every time and have gotten perfect results,” raves jtrzcinski. “This fudge is so creamy and delicious, it is requested by family and friends every year.”\n“I stuck to the recipe and it was wonderful,” says Chris Simmons. “My only suggestion: Place nuts in the oven for a quick roast/toast before adding them. Toasting the walnuts releases a warmer nutty flavor and made the fudge even more amazing, and offset some of the sweetness.”\n“This is super easy, but you have to watch it and stir constantly,” according to marybeth bellardini. “I didn’t make a single change and it comes out perfectly every single time. Definitely a keeper.”\nEditorial contributions by Corey Williams\nKeep Screen Awake\n## Ingredients\n1/2x\n[Selected: 1x]\n1x\n2x\nOriginal recipe (1X) yields 48 servings\n1 ½ cups white sugar\n1 (7 ounce) jar marshmallow creme\n2/3 cup evaporated milk\n1/4 cup butter\n1/4 teaspoon salt\n2 cups milk chocolate chips\n1 cup semisweet chocolate chips\n1/2 cup chopped nuts\n1 teaspoon vanilla extract\n## Directions\nGather all ingredients. Line an 8-inch square pan with aluminum foil; set aside.\nAllrecipes/Diana Chistruga\nCombine sugar, marshmallow cream, evaporated milk, butter, and salt together in a large saucepan over medium heat; bring to a full boil and cook for 5 minutes, stirring constantly.\nAllrecipes/Diana Chistruga\nRemove from heat and add milk chocolate chips and semisweet chocolate chips; stir until chocolate is melted and mixture is smooth. Stir in nuts and vanilla.\nAllrecipes/Diana Chistruga\nPour into prepared pan; chill in refrigerator for 2 hours, or until firm.\nAllrecipes/Diana Chistruga\nAllrecipes/Diana Chistruga\n### From the Editor\nPlease note differences in ingredient amounts and total time when following the Allrecipes magazine version of this recipe.\n## Nutrition Facts (per serving)\n[Table]\n124 | Calories\n6g | Fat\n18g | Carbs\n1g | Protein\n[End table]\nShow Full Nutrition Label\nCook this recipe and more in the MyRecipes app.\nGet the App\nSave\nRate\nPrint\n2,966\nhome cooks made it!\nI Made It\n## Ask the Community (7)\nAsked by BlueStraw3159\nIf I double the recipe, can I use 1-13oz jars of Marshmallow Creme instead of 2-7oz jars? Or does it have to be 14oz?\nView Answers (2)\nAsked by TwistyHam2107\nWhat is Marshmallow crème ? Can you get it in Australia\nView Answers (3)\nAsked by Jackie Tulchinsky Schroeder\nI made 2 batches all grainy. First I boiled for 5 min second even longer what am I doing wrong m\nView Answers (5)\nAsked by RDawson\nFirst time making fudge…what’s the best way to “prepare pan”?\nView Answers (1)\nLoad More questions\nGet recipe help and connect with the community\nAsk my question\n## Reviews (1,951)\nCheck out our Community Guidelines about reviews.\nAunt Teen’s Creamy Chocolate Fudge\nMy Rating\nRate this a 1\nRate this a 2\nRate this a 3\nRate this a 4\nRate this a 5\nMy Review\nCancel\nSubmit\n4.7 out of 5\n2465 Ratings\n5 star\n2016\n4 star\n266\n3 star\n81\n2 star\n47\n1 star\n55\n2,966\n home cooks made it!\nMost helpful positive review\nAllrecipes Member\n05/11/2006\n***PLEASE READ IF YOU ARE HAVING TROUBLE WITH CRUMBLY, GREASY FUDGE!*** I've used this recipe probably 50 times, and I've had it not turn out twice. The first time I used generic marshmallow cream. I don't recommend using any store brand ingredients in this fudge. The second time I used an enameled cast iron french oven, and it didn't turn out that time, either. Every time I use my calphalon, it turns out great. Also.....work fast! The faster the better. Don't start timing until the mixture is at a full, rolling boil and then reduce the temperature slightly so that it doesn't scorch. Stir the entire five minutes and have the chocolate chips measured out ahead of time so you can dump them in as soon as you turn off the heat and stir like mad. Get the chips fully incorporated and pour immediately. It never fails!\nRead More\n2,380\nReply\n### Featured Tweaks\nAdam M\n12/10/2005\nAbsolutely incredible fudge! It is perfectly smooth, creamy, chocolately, and all-around enjoyable. Try putting the marshmallow creme in the microwave for about 15 seconds before adding it to the pan; it will come out of the jar much easier. Though the cooking experience would have been less stressful with one, a candy thermometer really isn't necessary. One tablespoon of butter is just the right amount, and boiling the mixture for around 7 minutes instead of five can assure that the fudge will solidify. Some found that the recipe was a bit too sweet, so taking out 1/4 cup of sugar can tame some of the super-sweetness. Finally, using 2 cups of the semisweet chips and 1 cup of the milk chocolate chips instead of the other way around can make an even richer fudge (especially for those dark chocolate lovers like me). It is THE BEST fudge recipe around, and I am sure that I will make it again and again for years to come.\nRead More\n936\nAllrecipes Member\n11/26/2002\nThis was my first time to make fudge and it turned out great! Many people at my dessert buffet said that it was the best fudge they had ever had. It was so creamy - not grainy at all. I have to say, though, that I messed up the first time I tried it. Because I had the marshmallow creme in the very bottom of the pan and I got the heat too high too fast, I burned the marshmallow creme into hard brown chunks! The second time, I kept the temperature at low until it was mixed up well and then I turned the heat up to medium and boiled it. That worked like a charm.\nRead More\n598\nAllrecipes Member\n12/08/2002\nBasically, this represents a terrific, 5-star fudge recipe; I would tweak the instructions, though. First, cook for 6-1/2 minutes, because at 5 minutes, it really needs to be kept in the fridge to maintain the right texture. If you DO prefer the super-creaminess of the 5-minute cooking time, then it will be far less \"melty\" if, after refrigeration, you cut it into squares and let the squares \"air-harden\" on racks or trays until the sides are not tacky. (Obviously, this is kind of a pain, so I go with the 6-1/2 minutes!) And finally: This is too much butter. 1 tablespoon works well, and the butter can actually be left out completely if you like.\nRead More\n467\nJillian\n09/15/2010\nChocoholics Beware! This stuff is highly addictive! Sinfully rich, decadent, and creamy are just a few of the words that come to mind when describing this fudge. It's so good and so easy to make. I did cut the sugar back by 1/4 cup and used a non-stick pan which I highly recommend. Bring this to a boill slowly, stir constantly and you should have no problems. I lined my 8x8 pan with Reynolds non-stick foil and it came out perfect. This recipe is a definite keeper!\nRead More\n187\nChris Simmons\n12/20/2020\nI stuck to recipe and it was wonderful! My only suggestion: ALWAYS put nuts in the oven for a quick roast/toast before putting them in sweets. Toasting the walnuts before putting them in the fudge released a warmer nutty flavor and crisped the walnuts so that it made the fudge even more amazing, and offset some of the sweetness.\nI put the nuts in a pie plate and put them in 300 oven for 20 minutes, or until very fragrant. Don't let them go all the way to black, but a nice dark brown is ok.\nRead More\n5\nWobblyBrie9631\n12/13/2024\nThank you for sharing this recipe. Done right it is wonderful -- smooth and creamy. Frankly, I screwed it up the first time and it came out dense, grainy and crumbling. I think I cooked it for too long as I didn't start counting the \"boil\" until it was fully bubbling. I also think I used a pan that was too big for cooking the mixture.Due to the great reviews, I tried again and used a few of the tips from the other reviewers to see if I could get a better result. These are the things I did differently: 1) used a smaller pan; 2) used 3 T of butter (and I probably could have reduced further); 3) added a couple more Tablespoons of the evaporated milk to the recipe (to make it creamier); 4) doubled the vanilla to 2 t; 5) started timing the boil earlier in the process and only boiled 5 minutes; 6) waited to add the marshmallow cream until AFTER the boiling process; 7) used more semi-sweet chocolate to develop a deeper chocolate taste (I used 2 1/2 cups of semi-sweet and 1/2 cup of milk chocolate) and I melted it in the microwave first. The result was rich creamy fudge. I think the main things which caused the different result included the smaller pan, shorter boiling time (not over boiling) and adding the marshmallow cream later in the process.\nRead More\n0\nhowsefrau32\n12/21/2020\nI have made this recipe for the past ten years. It never fails and people always ask for it. Every time I try someone else's, I say to myself, not as good as the one I make. You have to wait to start timing for five minutes as soon as it comes to a rolling boil, stir constantly, don't use generic marshmallow creme, and have your chips and nuts etc ready to mix in right away. I use a full bag of Ghirardelli milk chocolate and half a bag of Ghirardelli semi sweet chips, they are much better than the cheaper ones. It's THE best fudge I have ever made or tasted.\nRead More\n2\nStrongRind3670\n12/08/2024\nThis is the perfect recipe for the best creamiest fudge ever. I think changing out the chocolate chips from 2 to one cup of chocolate chips and from 1 cup to 2 cups cups semi sweet chips is fine but when you start changing cooking time from 5 minutes as the recipe recommends to 6 -1/2 to 7 minutes, and changing how much canned milk you use and how much butter you use and changing the recommended marshmallow cream to marshmellows, etc then your just asking for dry crumbly none creamy fudge by changing up this perfect recipe. I just see a lot of this on here then people rudely saying the recipe is terrible or your disappointed and wont make it again is just rude, so if you want perfect fudge don't change up the recipe. Sorry but fudge is a recipe that can't be changed up too much or it will fail you. The author of this fudge recipe was kind and gracious enough to share an awesome and perfect fudge recipe with all of us and we should all just be thankful for that, and if you follow the recipe it should turn out perfect. If you dont have a candy thermometer please get one because that could be why your fudge didn't firm up or why it was crumbly. Thank you from the bottom of my heart, to the author of this awesome fudge recipe, for kindly sharing it with all of us.\nRead More\n5\nMomma Mophead\n01/05/2020\nI made a batch of this in the past and at that time I thought it was good, but I forgot to review.\nThis time around I made some changes, as my sweet tooth has changed. I guess old age does that?\nThe changes I made: Only used 1 cup sugar instead of 1 1/2 cup and added 1 cup frozen little marshmallows at the very end before folding into the prepped pan. I have been looking for a Rocky road fudge & couldn’t find one that suited me. So I altered this recipe as I knew I had liked it before altering it.\nThese changes did not alter the texture, its creamy & melts in your mouth.\nRead More\n3\nchappys_wife\n12/30/2023\nFollowed directions exactly and the fudge turned out crumbly and hard. The recipe needs a temperature for the ingredients to be boiled to, to hopefully give everyone consistent results.\nRead More\n3\n1951 Reviews\nSort\nFilter\nGene\n08/04/2026\nA keeper!\nDelicious, smooth , creamy and glossy. Every body loves it and when they ask for the recipe, I explain that its a well kept family secret .😁\n0\nReply\nMegan Spees McNeill\n01/02/2026\nA keeper!\nGreat flavors\nEasy to follow\nWorth the effort\nCrowd-pleaser\nFamily favorite\nMy husband described it as \"ridiculous\" (meaning that he loved it). Sorry, Grandma, but this will be my fudge recipe going forward.\n2\nReply\nKatelynn\n12/31/2025\nCrowd-pleaser\nSuper easy! My first time making fudge and it was really tasty! Gave the recipe out to a few friends, my mom and auntie. Thank you :)*** Followed the advice of not using store brand, used Jut-Puffed and Carnation for the milk and fluff***\n1\nReply\nLynn\n12/24/2025\nEasy to follow\nGreat flavors\nA keeper!\nWorth the effort\nCrowd-pleaser\nFamily favorite\nAwesome & Super easy!\n1\nReply\nlovejbo\n12/23/2025\nThis turned out great! Everyone loved it at multiple parties and it’s easy!! I used about 7 candy canes and just used chocolate chips.\n1\nReply\nStephen\n12/23/2025\nCrowd-pleaser\nI have been making this fudge for years , the friend and family ask for it every holiday season. It is a real crowd pleaser.\n1\nReply\nAdriann\n12/22/2025\nA keeper!\nEasy to follow\nCrowd-pleaser\nThis is my first time making fudge, but I’ve now used this recipe 3x and each time came out perfectly. I made the original chocolate, but then also tweaked it slightly to make 2 other flavors:- egg nog fudge (swap chocolate chips out for white chocolate chips, 2 tsp vanilla bean paste, 1 tsp ground nutmeg)- white chocolate mocha (swap chocolate chips out for white chocolate, 1 tbsp instant espresso powder, 2 tsp vanilla bean paste)I have ingredients to make one more batch and I’m excited to have such a great base to play around with flavors. This will definitely be my go-to recipe.\nRead More\n1\nReply\nRick\n12/20/2025\nOK with tweaks\nI have made it in the past without fail but I find it very very sweet. I am thinking about bypassing the sugar and evaporated milk and just using a lesser portion of sweetened condensed milk, and instead of milk chocolate I am going to go with Ghirardelli's 60% cacao dark chocolate chips mixed 50/50 with Ghirardelli's semi sweet.\n0\nReply\npsiwoman2\n12/20/2025\nA keeper!\nI have been looking for an easy, creamy fudge without the use of a candy thermometer. THIS IS IT!!! It came out perfect, even when I doubled the batch. Made a second batch using just milk chocolate-and I have now found my perfect fudge recipe!\n1\nReply\nLoad More reviews\n## Most-Saved Recipes Like This\nMillion Dollar Fudge\n222\n3 hrs 30 mins\nSave\nEasy Creamy Vanilla Fudge\n69\nSave\nMocha Fudge\n15\n25 mins\nSave\nGranddad's Peppermint Fudge\n2 hrs 35 mins\nSave\nThe Original Fantasy Fudge\n524\n1 hr 15 mins\nSave\nToll House Famous Fudge\n44\n2 hrs 20 mins\nSave\nChocolate Walnut Fudge\n115\n2 hrs\nSave\nPeanut Butter Fantasy Fudge\n4\n1 hr 30 mins\nSave\nOld-Fashioned Chocolate Fudge\n741\n1 hr 25 mins\nSave\nWhite Chocolate Fudge\n128\n1 hr 25 mins\nSave\n## You’ll Also Love\nMillion Dollar Fudge\n222\nRatings\nOld-Fashioned Chocolate Fudge\n741\nRatings\nCaramel Peanut Fudge\n150\nRatings\nGerman Chocolate Fudge\n167\nRatings\nCandy Bar Fudge\n76\nRatings\nCaramel Popcorn Balls\n196\nRatings\nPuppy Chow\n1,014\nRatings\nMy Amish Friend's Caramel Corn\n1,611\nRatings\nOreo Cookie Bark\n262\nRatings\nWorld's Best Peanut Butter Fudge\n616\nRatings\nPeppermint Brittle\n467\nRatings\nRolo Pretzel Turtles\n1,657\nRatings\nBonbons\n26\nRatings\nChocolate-Covered Caramels\n131\nRatings\nGrandpa's Popcorn Balls\n186\nRatings\nCreamy Peanut Butter Fudge\n1,149\nRatings\n--- frame: about:blank ---\n\n--- frame: about:blank ---\n\nImage descriptions: Squar\n[bytes 0-16384 of 17589; text from=16384 continues; the whole text is saved to $TMPDIR/bc-95t3sxow/w2/.browser-cmd/text-0001.txt]\n</browser>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
