{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'browser-cmd --help'","aggregated_output":"browser-cmd: a real Chrome for an agent, driven from the shell\n\nUsage:\n  browser-cmd <command> [args]     run one browser action against a warm daemon\n  browser-cmd status               is the daemon up\n  browser-cmd batch <<'EOF'        several commands, one per line, in one call: each\n                                   but the last says one line, the first failure stops\n  browser-cmd --serve [ADDR]       run the daemon in the foreground, in its workspace\n  browser-cmd <command> --help     one command's syntax\n\nCommands. Each prints the page state afterwards — after an action on the same\ndocument, only what changed — and a @ref from one command resolves in the next.\n--brief (or BROWSER_CMD_BRIEF=1) prints one line for an action that worked.\nArguments are words where the shape is obvious, and key=value otherwise; a lone\n'{json}' is passed through whole.\n\n  navigate URL [here=true] | back | forward\n      Open an http(s) URL in a real Chrome, or go back or forward.\n\n  snapshot [@ref] [full] [coords]\n      The page as its accessibility tree: controls and headings with @e1a.31\n      refs by default, everything readable with full.\n\n  click @ref | \"name\" | X,Y [double=true] [right=true] [again=true]\n      Click an element by ref or by the words on it, or a viewport point by x\n      and y, with a real pointer that travels there first.\n\n  hover @ref | \"name\" | X,Y\n      Move the pointer onto an element or point without clicking, for menus that\n      open on hover.\n\n  drag from='{…}' to='{…}'\n      Press on one element or point, travel to another with the button held, and\n      let go: a slider set, a card moved between columns, a map panned.\n\n  type [@ref] TEXT… [submit]\n      Replace the text of a field by typing it key by key, then press Enter if\n      asked to submit.\n\n  select @ref OPTION…\n      Choose an option in a native <select> by its text or value.\n\n  choose @ref TEXT… [option=SUGGESTION]\n      Fill an autocomplete or combobox: type TEXT, wait for its suggestions,\n      pick the one that matches (option= names it when TEXT is only what to\n      search for), and confirm the field took it.\n\n  press KEY\n      Press a key or chord such as Enter, Escape, Tab, PageDown, ArrowDown,\n      Control+a or Meta+a.\n\n  scroll [@ref] [up|down|left|right|bottom|top|TEXT]\n      Turn the wheel over the page, or scroll to an edge until the page stops\n      growing — which loads a whole infinite list in one call.\n\n  text [FIND] [ref=@ref] [from=N] [max_bytes=N] [links=true] [context=false] [--raw]\n      The rendered text of the page, bounded, as untrusted data.\n\n  images \n      Image URLs, alt text and sizes on the page, as untrusted data.\n\n  pages URL… [find=TEXT] [max_bytes=N] [keep=true] [full=true]\n      Read up to 12 pages in one call, as untrusted data: each opens in a tab of\n      its own, four at a time, settles and is read, then closes — the sources to\n      compare, the venues to list.\n\n  markup ref=@ref [levels=N]\n      The HTML of one element, and levels ancestors up, cleaned and bounded.\n\n  extract fields='{…}' [each=SEL] [all=true] [identity=SEL] [limit=N]\n      Read many like-shaped things in one call — every row of a table, every\n      card in a result list, every item of a feed — as JSON rows with a coverage\n      record.\n\n  screenshot [full | @ref | ref=@ref | selector=CSS | X,Y,W,H] [zoom=N]\n      A PNG of the viewport, of the full page, or of one element or section by\n      ref or CSS selector, returned as an image and saved under the workspace.\n\n  evidence \"VALUE\"…\n      Which of the given strings are visible in the viewport now, and where: x,y\n      and the element, or how many matches the page has out of view and the\n      nearest page y.\n\n  wait [SECONDS | TEXT] [gone=TEXT] [url=TEXT]\n      Wait for a page that draws itself: until text appears, until text\n      disappears, until the URL contains a string, or for a number of seconds\n      and the DOM to go quiet.\n\n  verify [text=…] [gone=…] [url=…] [ref=@ref]\n      Check that the page is now as expected and fail if it is not.\n\n  tab list|check|tidy | new [URL] | switch|close|keep|unkeep|give|restore [tN] | use N|ID\n      This session's tabs, each by a handle t1, t2, … it keeps for the session:\n      list them, switch to or close one, or open a new one at an optional url; a\n      window one of them opens later is this session's too.\n\n  upload ref=@ref path=PATH\n      Attach a file from the workspace to a file input, by ref.\n\n  eval EXPRESSION | - [--raw]\n      Evaluate a JavaScript expression in the page and return its JSON value; -\n      reads the expression from stdin, and --raw prints the value as one line of\n      JSON.\n\n  cdp METHOD ['{json params}']\n      The escape hatch: send a raw Chrome DevTools Protocol method to the tab\n      and get its result.\n\n  console [errors]\n      What the current tab logged — errors, blocked requests, CSP violations —\n      as untrusted data, for when a step did not do what was expected.\n\n  network [FIND] [body=true] [max_bytes=N]\n      The data the page fetched for itself — XHR, fetch, event-stream and socket\n      messages — as untrusted data: the JSON behind a map, a chart, a search or\n      a feed, which says more than the pixels drawn from it.\n\n  retry-clean [keep_signin=true]\n      Open the current URL again in a fresh isolated browser context with no\n      cookies or cache, when the profile's session, cache or reputation has\n      poisoned the page.\n\n  close \n      End this session: close its tabs, give back borrowed ones, and leave kept\n      ones open.\n\nThe daemon starts on first use and holds the session for this workspace\n(BROWSER_CMD_WORKSPACE, else the git work tree, else the working directory);\nBROWSER_CMD_ADDR picks its port. A failed command drops a trace under\n.browser-cmd/traces/ (BROWSER_CMD_NO_TRACE=1 to disable) and repeats its reason\non stderr. The browser is configured by BROWSER_CMD_* variables: PROFILE, CDP,\nEXECUTABLE, HEADLESS, NO_SANDBOX, ARGS, WINDOW, LANGUAGE, TIMEZONE, PROXY,\nPROXY_HOSTS, DIALOGS, PACE, NETWORK, NETWORK_NOTES and AWARENESS.\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
